 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2-1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358386 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358387 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358388 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358389 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358390 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1041 "selection" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358390 13194139535631 true] [17592186358385 1110 #uuid "396beca5-39ea-5f3b-a418-c8882472e609" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358386 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+3" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358386 1111 #uuid "03235b38-4b62-51da-b0fe-5cda64becd99" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358387 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-3" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358387 1111 #uuid "734f6b6c-d641-5eae-9eae-86eb1f9d01a9" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358388 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+3" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358388 1111 #uuid "7beb27be-16fb-5257-b777-20cfab38efc7" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358389 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358389 1111 #uuid "58885867-92b1-52af-a618-eb09284f397a" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358390 1034 "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358390 1111 #uuid "0bda7078-af52-5548-9659-2abf600878cf" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358392 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1089 "q-333251" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-xy,\\,y(0.5)=1.1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1113 #uuid "446684c1-6fd8-5e60-ae93-0058401c626a" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358391 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5(1){y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.1-0.5(1)^{3} \\\\  & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.6}{1.5}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1.1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.066667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.066667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358393 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358394 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1041 "field-1" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358393 13194139535631 true] [17592186358392 1110 #uuid "f9fbf995-7485-5d67-afa9-849bbd8afcf6" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358393 1034 "1.07" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358393 1111 #uuid "c2928918-59fc-547d-b0d8-de95f5d79ab3" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358394 1034 "1.05" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535631 true] [17592186358394 1111 #uuid "f4e4a0d3-285f-5b28-b557-4cc20ea03ad6" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358396 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1089 "q-333298" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3x,\\,y(1.5)=-2.5\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1113 #uuid "875d5bc3-0a8e-5296-bc39-00195d13f0a5" 13194139535631 true] [17592186358395 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1.5+0.5=2.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.5-1.5(2) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-2.5$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5.5}{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})-1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5)=-2.5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-2.5 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =-2.464286 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-2.464102 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-2.464102\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-2.46,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535631 true] [17592186358396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358397 13194139535631 true] [17592186358396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535631 true] [17592186358396 1041 "field-1" 13194